      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-2024-june/q15",
      "question": "<ol class=\"parts\" type=\"i\"><li>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k^2-4k+5\"></span> is positive for all real values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li><li>A student was asked to prove by contradiction that<p>“There are no positive integers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> such that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3x+2y)(2x-5y)=28\"></span>”</p><p>The start of the student’s proof is shown below.</p><div style=\"border:1px solid #9aa5ae;padding:2mm 4mm;margin:2mm auto;max-width:120mm;text-align:center\"><p>Assume that positive integers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> exist such that</p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(3x+2y)(2x-5y)=28\"></span></p><p>If <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3x+2y=14\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2x-5y=2\"></span></p><p><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left.\\begin{aligned}3x+2y&amp;=14\\\\2x-5y&amp;=2\\end{aligned}\\right\\}\\Rightarrow x=\\dfrac{74}{19},\\ y=\\dfrac{22}{19}\\quad\\text{Not integers}\"></span></p></div><p>Show the calculations and statements needed to complete the proof.</p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol>",
      "steps": [
        "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-01-2024-june/q15-ms-p35-1.svg\" alt=\"Official mark scheme for original question 15; source page 35, segment 1 of 2. Includes the original working, marks, alternative methods and examiner guidance in source order.\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:100%\"></figure>",
